
 Changes
 =======
  - bitnami  is not available anymore I will use spark offical image
  - We will use Apache/Spark offcial image
  - not there two versions
    * Hardened Images Apache Spark - optimize for secure usage - WE DON'T use it
    * normal Apache Spark image - https://hub.docker.com/r/apache/spark

  - I have created a docker-compose.yml 

TODO
====
--------------------------------------
  1. start container in the background
--------------------------------------
      $ docker compose up -d  
      NOTE: you need to shutdown for update to take place docker compose down
    1.2. Test if Container is running
      $ docker ps
      Expected output:  you should see two container running
    1.3.  TEST if the UI is up 
      in your browser paste (Master UI) http://localhost:8080 (Worker UI) http://localhost:8081
    1.4. TEST if spak is running - use the PI example provieded in the spark release
     - get the version of the examples jar file to run a test : 
       docker exec <spark-image-id> ls /opt/spark/examples/jars/ 
     - check the version of the jar files and if needed update the follwing command, from the host machine: 
       $ docker exec -it spark-spark-1 /opt/spark/bin/spark-submit \
                                 --master spark://spark-spark-1:7077 \
                                 --class org.apache.spark.examples.SparkPi \
                                 /opt/spark/examples/jars/spark-examples.jar 10
      - Expected output: you should see the value of PI 3.14.....  (look carefully there are lot of information printed on the shell)
--------------------------------
  2. Add volume to the containes
--------------------------------
    2.1  update the docker-compose as indicated in the guidelines
    2.2. shutdown the containers and restart 
      $ docker compose up down
      $ docker compose up -d
    2.3 Test the volume is attached correctly 
      $ docker exec <spark-image-id> ls /opt/spark-data
      - Expected output you should see the file in the directory of the mount point (your host current directoy )
------------------------
  3. Run Analytics scala
------------------------
    3.1 download kaggle data 
     curl -L -o ~/Downloads/bank-personal-loan-modelling.zip https://www.kaggle.com/api/v1/datasets/download/krantiswalke/bank-personal-loan-modelling
    3.2 get an inteactive shell in the container: 
       $ docker exec -it <spark-image-id> bash
       - in the container: 
       $ cd to the /opt/spark 
    3.3 start Scala interpreter
       $ ./bin/spark-shell --master spark://spark-spark-1:7077
       Expected output: you should see the scale interpreter starting
    3.4 run the scala command as indicated in the tutorial 
      Expeted output val df: org.apache.spark.sql.DataFrame = [ID: string, Age: string ... 12 more fields]
--------------------------
4. Run analytics in python
--------------------------
    3.1 download kaggle data 
     curl -L -o ~/Downloads/bank-personal-loan-modelling.zip https://www.kaggle.com/api/v1/datasets/download/krantiswalke/bank-personal-loan-modelling
    3.2 get an inteactive shell in the container: 
       $ docker exec -it <spark-image-id> bash
       - in the container: 
       $ cd to the /opt/spark 
    3.3 start python interpreter
       $ opt/spark/bin/pyspark --master spark://spark-spark-1:7077
       Expected output: you should see the python interpreter starting
    3.4 run the python command in sparkshell
       >>> df = spark.read.format("csv").option("header", "true").option("inferSchema", "true").load("/opt/spark-data/Bank_Personal_Loan_Modelling.csv")
       >>> df.printSchema()
       >>> df.printSchema()
       >>> df.select("Age", "Income", "Family").filter(df.Age > 30).show(10)
      Expeted output val df: org.apache.spark.sql.DataFrame = [ID: string, Age: string ... 12 more fields]
    3.5 ALTERNATIVE: save the command in file and run the following
     - from the host : docker exec -it <spark-image-id> /opt/spark/bin/spark-submit  --master spark://spark-spark-1:7077
     - from the container: ./bin/spark-submit --master spark://spark-spark-1:7077 /opt/spark-data/data-analytics.py
---------------
5. Scaling test
---------------
  5.1 change the docker-compose to scale the cluster 
      - in the spark_worker:  commentout the namecontainer 
      - in the spark_worker:  change port mapping from "8080":"8080"  to "8080" this will make open for multiple instances
  5.2 rerun step 3 or 4 
      - Test through the UI paste in the browser (Master UI) http://localhost:8080
      -  you  will see the master UI click on the Application section: you will three works have excuted 
      
